# GITHUB LESSON 21 — WHAT GITHUB ACTUALLY IS

=== PART 1 ===
GitHub is a website that stores your work online.

=== PART 2 ===
GitHub keeps your work safe even if your computer breaks.

=== PART 3 ===
GitHub organizes your work into “projects.”

=== PART 4 ===
A project on GitHub is called a “repository.”

=== PART 5 ===
A repository is simply a container for your files.

=== PART 6 ===
GitHub does not change your work. It only stores it.

=== PART 7 ===
GitHub shows your files in a clean, readable layout.

=== PART 8 ===
GitHub keeps a history of your changes.

=== PART 9 ===
GitHub lets you see older versions of your work.

=== PART 10 ===
GitHub lets you share your work with others.

=== PART 11 ===
GitHub helps teams work on the same project without overwriting each other.

=== PART 12 ===
GitHub uses Git to track changes.

=== PART 13 ===
Git is the memory system. GitHub is the website that stores that memory.

=== PART 14 ===
GitHub shows your Git history visually.

=== PART 15 ===
GitHub lets you create new files directly on the website.

=== PART 16 ===
GitHub lets you upload files from your computer.

=== PART 17 ===
GitHub lets you download your entire project at any time.

=== PART 18 ===
GitHub is the safest place to store long‑term work.

=== PART 19 ===
GitHub is the standard tool used by developers and creators.

=== PART 20 ===
